※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A name written with a mark after it -- `T'` -- is a type a call settles
※ rather than a type.  It is declared by being used: what a function's type
※ parameters are is which marked names its parameters mention, so there is no
※ list to write and none to keep in step with the parameters.
※
※ **The types come from the arguments.**  Each argument is lowered where it
※ stands, as any call's is, and what it turns out to be says more about the
※ types -- so an argument is lowered knowing what the ones to its left said,
※ and a literal with no suffix takes the type an earlier argument settled.
※
※ **The body is checked where it is written**, against its requirements alone:
※ an operator or a function applied to a value of a type parameter is one a
※ `pre` clause names (4645), so a call is told in the signature what its types
※ must support.  Taking a value apart by the shape the parameter is written
※ with -- an element of `T'⟦⟧`, what `&T'` names, a member of a tuple -- and
※ calling a lambda the function was handed ask nothing of the type.
※
※ One function is made per set of types and not one per call: a second call
※ saying what an earlier one said gets that same function.

fn largest(a: T', b: T') → T' pre(T' ⌈ T' → T'):
    a ⌈ b

※ The mark may stand inside a type rather than as the whole of it, and what
※ the argument is read against is the shape it was written with.
fn first(v: T'⟦⟧) → T':
    v⟦⎕unit(0u64, ⌜idx⌝)⟧

fn through(r: &T') → T':
    r⌖

fn left(p: 〈T', U'〉) → T':
    p⟦0⟧

fn right(p: 〈T', U'〉) → U':
    p⟦1⟧

※ A generic function may take a lambda, and a lambda's type may mention a type
※ parameter like any other type.
@[impure]
fn applied(g: fn(T') → T', x: T') → T':
    g(g(x))

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let v: u8⟦3⟧ = ⟦7u8, 8u8, 9u8⟧ in ⎕static
    let w: u16⟦2⟧ = ⟦40u16, 50u16⟧ in ⎕static
    let n: u16 = 4u16
    ※ one function, two sets of types
    if largest(1u8, 2u8) ≠ 2u8:
        1u6
    elif largest(5u32, 3u32) ≠ 5u32:
        2u6
    ※ a literal with no suffix, taking the type the first argument settled
    elif largest(9u8, 4) ≠ 9u8:
        3u6
    ※ read out of an array, whose length the call lets go of
    elif first(v) ≠ 7u8 ∨ first(w) ≠ 40u16:
        4u6
    ※ and out of a reference
    elif through(&n) ≠ 4u16:
        5u6
    ※ two type parameters, settled by the two members of one tuple
    elif left(〈1u8, 2u16〉) ≠ 1u8 ∨ right(〈1u8, 2u16〉) ≠ 2u16:
        6u6
    ※ a lambda handed to one, which is a value like any other
    elif applied(λ a: u8 → u8 { a + 1u8 }, 5u8) ≠ 7u8:
        7u6
    ※ the same types again, which is the same function and not another
    elif largest(1u8, 3u8) ≠ 3u8:
        8u6
    else:
        0u6
